1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,472
Your CTO asks: "git log
dates this change 24 August.

2
00:00:08,512 --> 00:00:13,024
The release notes list it under
'merged since 1 September'.

3
00:00:13,064 --> 00:00:14,799
Which system is wrong?"

4
00:00:15,139 --> 00:00:20,046
And a second question the same week: "Half
of our release manager's commits appear

5
00:00:20,086 --> 00:00:23,787
on GitHub without her avatar,
under a name that is not a link.

6
00:00:23,827 --> 00:00:27,649
Did somebody else push them?" Make
your guess on both, out loud.

7
00:00:32,489 --> 00:00:37,603
Neither system is wrong, and nobody else
pushed. A commit, one saved snapshot of the

8
00:00:37,643 --> 00:00:42,572
project, records two people and two times,
and Git prints one date and filters by

9
00:00:42,612 --> 00:00:47,604
the other. And Git copies a name and an
email address from configuration into each

10
00:00:47,644 --> 00:00:52,512
commit, and checks neither. Today is about
the two identity lines and the parent

11
00:00:52,552 --> 00:00:57,666
lines of a commit. You'll reproduce the
first puzzle on screen, and solve the second

12
00:00:57,706 --> 00:00:58,709
before we finish.

13
00:01:00,549 --> 00:01:04,235
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:04,275 --> 00:01:08,878
In the last video you read a commit
object: tree, parent, author, committer,

15
00:01:08,918 --> 00:01:12,299
message. Today we take the
middle three lines seriously.

16
00:01:12,639 --> 00:01:17,034
First, author and committer: who they are,
when they differ, and what that does to

17
00:01:17,074 --> 00:01:21,468
dates in a log. Then a commit made with
the wrong identity: how to diagnose it and

18
00:01:21,508 --> 00:01:25,902
fix the cause. Then parents: root commits,
ordinary commits and merge commits, and

19
00:01:25,942 --> 00:01:29,255
the two suffixes, caret and tilde,
that walk the parent links.

20
00:01:31,095 --> 00:01:32,673
Here's what you'll walk away with.

21
00:01:33,013 --> 00:01:34,426
After this video you can:

22
00:01:34,766 --> 00:01:38,941
Distinguish author from committer, and name
three operations that make them differ.

23
00:01:39,281 --> 00:01:43,861
Say which date git log prints, and
which date --since filters on.

24
00:01:44,201 --> 00:01:47,854
Diagnose a commit made with the
wrong identity, and fix the cause.

25
00:01:48,194 --> 00:01:51,582
Navigate the parents of
a merge with ^ and ~.

26
00:01:53,422 --> 00:01:57,865
Author and committer. In one sentence:
the author is the person who wrote the

27
00:01:57,905 --> 00:02:01,998
change, and the committer is the
person who created this commit object.

28
00:02:02,038 --> 00:02:06,539
Each is recorded with a name, an email
address, a time and a time-zone offset.

29
00:02:06,879 --> 00:02:12,548
Where do the values come from? For both
identities, Git reads the environment first,

30
00:02:12,588 --> 00:02:17,917
the variables your shell hands to a
program: GIT_AUTHOR_NAME, GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL,

31
00:02:17,957 --> 00:02:21,723
GIT_AUTHOR_DATE, and the three
GIT_COMMITTER_ variables.

32
00:02:21,763 --> 00:02:25,393
Then user.name and user.email,
from the configuration.

33
00:02:25,433 --> 00:02:30,287
And as a last resort it guesses from
the system user name and host name.

34
00:02:30,327 --> 00:02:34,093
--author and --date set the
author fields of one commit.

35
00:02:34,133 --> 00:02:39,598
The committer has no such option: only the
environment, the configuration and the

36
00:02:39,638 --> 00:02:40,006
clock.

37
00:02:40,346 --> 00:02:43,441
git commit: author you,
now. Committer you, now.

38
00:02:43,781 --> 00:02:48,521
git commit --author=...
--date=...: author as given.

39
00:02:48,561 --> 00:02:50,268
Committer you, now.

40
00:02:50,608 --> 00:02:55,676
git cherry-pick, git rebase, git commit
--amend: author copied from the original

41
00:02:55,716 --> 00:03:00,210
commit. Committer you, now. Those are
the three operations to remember.

42
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:05,383
Cherry-pick copies a commit, rebase replays
a branch, and amend replaces the last

43
00:03:05,423 --> 00:03:07,426
commit. Each gets its own video.

44
00:03:07,766 --> 00:03:12,865
git commit --amend --reset-author:
you, now, for both.

45
00:03:13,205 --> 00:03:18,364
git am, applying a mailed patch: the
author's name from the mail's From: line and

46
00:03:18,404 --> 00:03:21,509
the date from its Date:
line. Committer you, now.

47
00:03:21,849 --> 00:03:26,793
Quick quiz, three options. You rebase a
branch that a colleague wrote last month.

48
00:03:26,833 --> 00:03:30,977
On the new commits, who is the
author: you, your colleague, or both?

49
00:03:31,017 --> 00:03:31,961
Say your answer.

50
00:03:36,801 --> 00:03:41,055
Your colleague. The author is copied from
the original commit, and you become the

51
00:03:41,095 --> 00:03:42,592
committer, with today's time.

52
00:03:42,932 --> 00:03:47,719
Times and zones. The seconds identify the
instant. The offset only says what the

53
00:03:47,759 --> 00:03:52,606
wall clock showed where the commit was
made. By default Git prints each date with

54
00:03:52,646 --> 00:03:53,812
its recorded offset.

55
00:03:54,152 --> 00:03:59,127
Which date Git uses where. This is the
root cause of the hook, in the textbook's

56
00:03:59,167 --> 00:04:04,266
words: git log prints the author date, and
--since and --until select by committer

57
00:04:04,306 --> 00:04:09,343
date. A listing of "everything since the
first of September" therefore contains a

58
00:04:09,383 --> 00:04:14,608
commit that displays the 24th of August.
Both are correct: the change was written in

59
00:04:14,648 --> 00:04:17,491
August, and entered this
history in September.

60
00:04:17,831 --> 00:04:22,625
Why two dates? Git needs the committer
date for its own work: the manual says the

61
00:04:22,665 --> 00:04:26,981
history-walking machinery assumes that
commits have non-decreasing commit

62
00:04:27,021 --> 00:04:31,993
timestamps. The author date is for people:
it survives every rebase and cherry-pick.

63
00:04:32,333 --> 00:04:38,005
Identity is an assertion. Git copies name
and email into the commit and checks

64
00:04:38,045 --> 00:04:43,936
neither. When a repository-local user.email
is left over from another project, it

65
00:04:43,976 --> 00:04:49,281
overrides the global one, and every
commit in that repository carries it.

66
00:04:49,321 --> 00:04:54,699
git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT prints the
identity the next commit would record.

67
00:04:54,739 --> 00:05:00,850
git config get --show-scope --show-origin
--all user.email names the file each value

68
00:05:00,890 --> 00:05:06,781
comes from. get needs Git 2.46 or later.
And user.useConfigOnly turns Git's guess

69
00:05:06,821 --> 00:05:07,806
into an error.

70
00:05:13,146 --> 00:05:18,299
Parents. In one sentence: the parent lines
of a commit are the only links in the

71
00:05:18,339 --> 00:05:23,751
history graph: none for a root commit, one
for an ordinary commit, two or more for a

72
00:05:23,791 --> 00:05:28,489
merge commit. In the picture, d4c9fab
is the root and ae6795c is a merge.

73
00:05:28,829 --> 00:05:33,836
Parents are ordered. The first parent is
the commit HEAD pointed at when the commit

74
00:05:33,876 --> 00:05:38,882
was made, HEAD being the commit you're on.
For a merge, the further parents are the

75
00:05:38,922 --> 00:05:42,104
commits you merged in. Two
suffixes walk these links.

76
00:05:42,144 --> 00:05:46,907
^<n> selects the n-th parent of one commit.
~<n> follows first parents n times.

77
00:05:46,947 --> 00:05:51,892
Caret chooses among the parents of one
commit. Tilde goes back in a straight line.

78
00:05:52,232 --> 00:05:57,867
A repository can have more than one root:
git switch --orphan starts a history with

79
00:05:57,907 --> 00:06:02,721
no parent, and git merge
--allow-unrelated-histories joins two of them.

80
00:06:03,061 --> 00:06:07,879
Risk labels in this video. git cherry-pick
is 🟡 CAUTION: it adds one commit to the

81
00:06:07,919 --> 00:06:12,738
current branch. git commit --amend is 🟡
CAUTION: it replaces the tip commit with a

82
00:06:12,778 --> 00:06:17,715
new one. git config unset is 🟡 CAUTION: it
changes one configuration file, which has

83
00:06:17,755 --> 00:06:22,573
no history. git merge is 🟡 CAUTION: it
moves the current branch and rewrites index

84
00:06:22,613 --> 00:06:23,580
and working tree.

85
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:29,188
When not to rewrite an identity. For
the last commit, unpublished: --amend

86
00:06:29,228 --> 00:06:35,069
--reset-author. For published commits, the
textbook's fix column says: leave them.

87
00:06:36,909 --> 00:06:41,382
Think of a letter and its envelope. The
author line is the signature inside the

88
00:06:41,422 --> 00:06:45,668
letter: who wrote it, and the date they
wrote it. The committer line is the

89
00:06:45,708 --> 00:06:49,096
postmark: who put it into this
particular history, and when.

90
00:06:49,136 --> 00:06:53,838
Forward the letter, which is what a cherry-pick
or a rebase does, and it gets a new

91
00:06:53,878 --> 00:06:55,952
postmark. The signature inside stays.

92
00:06:56,292 --> 00:07:00,379
git log shows you the date on the
letter. --since sorts by the postmark.

93
00:07:00,719 --> 00:07:04,770
Where it breaks: neither the signature
nor the postmark is verified.

94
00:07:04,810 --> 00:07:07,958
Both are text supplied by
whoever creates the commit.

95
00:07:07,998 --> 00:07:12,289
A real postmark is stamped by a trusted
office. A committer line is not.

96
00:07:12,629 --> 00:07:17,959
For parents, keep the family tree from video
9: each record lists the parents, none

97
00:07:17,999 --> 00:07:19,188
lists the children.

98
00:07:21,028 --> 00:07:25,015
Try it now, on paper, thirty
seconds. Copy this graph.

99
00:07:25,055 --> 00:07:31,055
HEAD is the merge commit, ae6795c. Mark
the commit that HEAD^2 names, and the one

100
00:07:31,095 --> 00:07:34,858
that HEAD~2 names. Pause
me, and write your answer.

101
00:07:39,698 --> 00:07:46,719
Start at HEAD, the merge commit ae6795c.
HEAD^1, also written HEAD^ or HEAD~1, is

102
00:07:46,759 --> 00:07:54,042
its first parent, 191bbd1: where main was.
HEAD^2 is its second parent, 59c914e: the

103
00:07:54,082 --> 00:08:01,017
tip of the merged branch. HEAD~2 goes two
steps along first parents, to bb904cd.

104
00:08:01,057 --> 00:08:06,509
And HEAD^2~1 is the first parent
of the second parent, 79ff6d7.

105
00:08:06,549 --> 00:08:11,129
HEAD~2 and HEAD^^ are the
same commit. HEAD^2 is not.

106
00:08:11,169 --> 00:08:15,314
If you mixed the two up,
you're in good company.

107
00:08:17,154 --> 00:08:21,119
Asha wrote a commit on her branch.
You cherry-pick it onto main.

108
00:08:21,159 --> 00:08:26,063
git cherry-pick 🟡 CAUTION. Predict the
four lines of --format=fuller on the new

109
00:08:26,103 --> 00:08:31,069
commit: who is the author, who is the
committer, and which time does each carry?

110
00:08:31,109 --> 00:08:32,758
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

111
00:08:40,599 --> 00:08:45,945
The new commit, 0cf479c, keeps Asha as
author with her time, and records you as

112
00:08:45,985 --> 00:08:48,058
committer, three minutes later.

113
00:08:48,398 --> 00:08:54,473
The author can also be set by hand, here
for a file that Ravi mailed from another

114
00:08:54,513 --> 00:08:56,814
time zone: --author and --date.

115
00:08:57,154 --> 00:09:01,850
Three views of the same two
instants. With the recorded offsets.

116
00:09:01,890 --> 00:09:08,065
With --date=iso-local, converted to your
zone: Ravi's half past nine in the morning,

117
00:09:08,105 --> 00:09:13,393
at UTC minus 7, is ten in the evening
at UTC plus five and a half hours.

118
00:09:13,433 --> 00:09:16,427
And with --date=unix, the stored seconds.

119
00:09:16,767 --> 00:09:20,392
Contribution counts depend on
which identity is counted.

120
00:09:20,432 --> 00:09:23,207
By author, three people.
By committer, one.

121
00:09:26,547 --> 00:09:30,502
An amend 🟡 CAUTION keeps the
author and renews the committer.

122
00:09:30,542 --> 00:09:33,384
--reset-author makes
you the author as well.

123
00:09:33,724 --> 00:09:37,778
A commit written on the 24th of August
and cherry-picked on the seventh of

124
00:09:37,818 --> 00:09:38,332
September.

125
00:09:38,672 --> 00:09:45,303
Plain git log shows the 24th of August.
The format line shows both: authored in

126
00:09:45,343 --> 00:09:52,144
August, committed in September. Now the
filter. Does --since=2026-09-01 list this

127
00:09:52,184 --> 00:09:55,100
commit? Yes or no. Say your answer.

128
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:06,519
Yes, it does. And the last command
prints the trap in one line: selected by

129
00:10:06,559 --> 00:10:11,285
--since=2026-09-01, displayed
with the date 24 August.

130
00:10:11,325 --> 00:10:15,168
That's the CTO's first
question, reproduced.

131
00:10:18,008 --> 00:10:22,164
The new commit carries a different
email address from the one before it.

132
00:10:22,204 --> 00:10:26,769
Diagnose before you fix. Which two commands
tell you what the next commit would

133
00:10:26,809 --> 00:10:29,042
record, and where the value comes from?

134
00:10:29,382 --> 00:10:35,700
git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT prints the
identity. git config get --show-scope

135
00:10:35,740 --> 00:10:42,667
--show-origin --all user.email shows two
values: a global one and a local one in

136
00:10:42,707 --> 00:10:45,454
.git/config. The local one wins.

137
00:10:45,794 --> 00:10:51,219
Fix the cause first: git config unset
🟡 CAUTION removes the wrong setting.

138
00:10:51,259 --> 00:10:56,684
Then rewrite the unpublished commit
with --amend --no-edit --reset-author.

139
00:10:57,024 --> 00:11:02,491
With user.useConfigOnly=true and no
email configured, Git refuses instead of

140
00:11:02,531 --> 00:11:03,144
guessing.

141
00:11:05,984 --> 00:11:12,313
%p prints the parents. The oldest commit
has none, and git rev-list --max-parents=0

142
00:11:12,353 --> 00:11:13,003
finds it.

143
00:11:13,343 --> 00:11:18,616
git merge --no-ff 🟡 CAUTION. A merge
commit is an ordinary commit object with a

144
00:11:18,656 --> 00:11:23,121
second parent line. Its tree is a
complete snapshot like any other.

145
00:11:26,461 --> 00:11:32,509
Check your paper answers against the
real output: HEAD^1, HEAD^2, HEAD~2 and

146
00:11:32,549 --> 00:11:38,756
HEAD^2~1. And HEAD^3? This merge has two
parents, so there's no third, and the

147
00:11:38,796 --> 00:11:40,999
command exits with status 1.

148
00:11:41,339 --> 00:11:46,274
A merge commit has one diff per parent.
Against the first parent, you see what the

149
00:11:46,314 --> 00:11:51,249
merge brought into main. Against the second,
what main had that the feature branch

150
00:11:51,289 --> 00:11:55,982
lacked. --first-parent lists what happened
to main itself, one line per merge.

151
00:12:05,822 --> 00:12:07,470
Five mistakes to watch for.

152
00:12:07,810 --> 00:12:11,893
Calling a commit dated last month
in "changes since Monday" a bug.

153
00:12:11,933 --> 00:12:17,016
Root cause: git log prints the author date,
and --since selects by committer date.

154
00:12:17,356 --> 00:12:20,333
Reading "author differs from
committer" as tampering.

155
00:12:20,373 --> 00:12:25,000
Root cause: cherry-pick, rebase and amend
copy the author and renew the committer;

156
00:12:25,040 --> 00:12:28,358
commits created on GitHub carry
a GitHub committer address.

157
00:12:28,698 --> 00:12:35,019
Commits carry the wrong name or email.
Root cause: a repository-local user.email

158
00:12:35,059 --> 00:12:41,617
overrides the global one; git config get
--show-origin --all user.email shows both.

159
00:12:41,957 --> 00:12:47,506
Using HEAD^2 where HEAD~2 was meant. Root
cause: caret selects among the parents of

160
00:12:47,546 --> 00:12:50,200
one commit, tilde follows first parents.

161
00:12:50,540 --> 00:12:55,821
Taking the line below a merge in git log
for the previous state of the branch.

162
00:12:55,861 --> 00:13:01,415
Root cause: plain git log interleaves both
sides of a merge by date; use HEAD^1 or

163
00:13:01,455 --> 00:13:02,438
--first-parent.

164
00:13:04,278 --> 00:13:09,096
Now, out of the lab. A team builds its
weekly report with git log --since.

165
00:13:09,136 --> 00:13:14,478
After a large branch is rebased and merged,
the report counts every commit of that

166
00:13:14,518 --> 00:13:18,547
branch as new, while plain git
log shows dates from weeks ago.

167
00:13:18,887 --> 00:13:24,263
The textbook explains it: after a rebase,
every commit of the branch has the rebase

168
00:13:24,303 --> 00:13:28,178
time as committer date and the
original time as author date.

169
00:13:28,218 --> 00:13:33,333
Its advice: for release notes, select by
a range between two tags, not by date.

170
00:13:33,373 --> 00:13:36,660
In an audit, print both
dates with --format=fuller.

171
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:42,634
And the release manager from the hook? Her
commits were made in a repository with a

172
00:13:42,674 --> 00:13:48,239
leftover local user.email that matches no
address on her GitHub account, so GitHub

173
00:13:48,279 --> 00:13:53,092
can't link them. Nobody else pushed.
The prevention the textbook lists:

174
00:13:53,132 --> 00:13:58,697
user.useConfigOnly=true, and per-directory
identity with includeIf, which video 28

175
00:13:58,737 --> 00:13:59,176
covers.

176
00:14:01,016 --> 00:14:07,298
Your turn. Do Exercise 3.2, Level
1, "author and committer", in

177
00:14:07,338 --> 00:14:10,610
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

178
00:14:10,950 --> 00:14:16,317
Before each operation in the exercise,
predict the four values of --format=fuller:

179
00:14:16,357 --> 00:14:19,416
author, author date,
committer, committer date.

180
00:14:21,256 --> 00:14:26,313
Q32: "Explain author and committer with
three operations that make them differ.

181
00:14:26,353 --> 00:14:30,378
Which date does git log print,
and which one does --since use?"

182
00:14:32,718 --> 00:14:37,509
Answer out loud. A strong answer defines
the two roles by what each person did,

183
00:14:37,549 --> 00:14:42,646
names three operations and says what each
keeps and what it renews, and then answers

184
00:14:42,686 --> 00:14:47,109
the two date questions and explains
why Git needs the second date at all.

185
00:14:47,149 --> 00:14:49,433
End with the consequence for a report.

186
00:14:51,273 --> 00:14:56,681
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say, in your own words: the author wrote

187
00:14:56,721 --> 00:15:00,358
the change and the committer
created this commit object.

188
00:15:00,398 --> 00:15:05,806
Each has a name, an email, a time and an
offset. Cherry-pick, rebase and amend keep

189
00:15:05,846 --> 00:15:10,204
the author and renew the committer.
git log prints the author date.

190
00:15:10,244 --> 00:15:15,259
--since and --until filter on the committer
date. Identity is copied from the

191
00:15:15,299 --> 00:15:18,869
environment or the configuration
and checked by nobody.

192
00:15:18,909 --> 00:15:23,464
git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT and git
config get --show-origin diagnose it.

193
00:15:23,804 --> 00:15:29,510
A commit has zero, one or several ordered
parents. ^n picks the n-th parent and ~n

194
00:15:29,550 --> 00:15:31,052
walks n first parents.

195
00:15:32,892 --> 00:15:36,647
Read sections 6.5 and 6.6 of Chapter 6.

196
00:15:36,987 --> 00:15:44,220
Do Exercise 3.3, Level 1, "zero, one and
two parents", and Exercise 3.5, Level 2,

197
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:51,404
"which date is shown, which date filters?",
in exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

198
